  "query_preview": "..\n\nThe rest of today through Wednesday...\n\nScattered (30-60% chance) showers and thunderstorms will begin \nover the west this afternoon, moving east before gradually \ndissipating late this evening. A few storms may become strong to \nsevere, with strong winds of 60+ mph. Any storm will be capable of\nproducing small hail and pockets of heavy rain. SPC has issued a \nMarginal Risk (level 1 of 5) for much of western and central \nMontana and a Slight Risk (level 2 of 5) for a portion of western \nMontana.\n\nConditions will remain hot and dry ahead of the showers and \nthunderstorms moving in. Take care not to cause a spark!\n\nFor Friday and Saturday, an upper level trough approaches from \nthe Pacific Northwest before sliding across northern Montana \nSunday into early Monday. Moisture will be elevated, with PWATs \naround 140-180% of normal, allowing scattered to numerous (40-80% \nchance) showers and thunderstorms to continue in the afternoon and\nevening hours through the weekend, with potential for showers to \nlinger into the overnight hours (20-50% chance). A few storms may \nbecome strong to severe, although which day will have the most \npotential will depend on the timing of the bulk of the energy \nassociated with the trough moving through. This will be something \nto keep an eye on. Additionally, temperatures will gradually cool \nthrough the weekend, decreasing to low 70s to low 80s by Sunday.\n\nGoing into next week, conditions look to remain unsettled under \nupper level southwest flow. This will keep highs near to slightly \nabove normal (generally 80s) and allow a low daily chance (10-40%)\nfor afternoon and evening showers and thunderstorms to continue. \nArcher",
